NNAAMMEE
       last, lastb - show a listing of last logged in users

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       llaasstt [options] [_u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e...] [_t_t_y...]

       llaassttbb [options] [_u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e...] [_t_t_y...]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       llaasstt searches back through the _/_v_a_r_/_l_o_g_/_w_t_m_p file (or the file
       designated by the --ff option) and displays a list of all users logged in
       (and out) since that file was created. One or more _u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e_s and/or
       _t_t_y_s can be given, in which case llaasstt will show only the entries
       matching those arguments. Names of _t_t_y_s can be abbreviated, thus llaasstt 00
       is the same as llaasstt ttttyy00.

       When catching a SSIIGGIINNTT signal (generated by the interrupt key, usually
       control-C) or a SSIIGGQQUUIITT signal, llaasstt will show how far it has searched
       through the file; in the case of the SSIIGGIINNTT signal llaasstt will then
       terminate.

       The pseudo user rreebboooott logs in each time the system is rebooted. Thus
       llaasstt rreebboooott will show a log of all the reboots since the log file was
       created.

       llaassttbb is the same as llaasstt, except that by default it shows a log of the
       _/_v_a_r_/_l_o_g_/_b_t_m_p file, which contains all the bad login attempts.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --aa, ----hhoossttllaasstt
           Display the hostname in the last column. Useful in combination with
           the ----ddnnss option.

       --dd, ----ddnnss
           For non-local logins, Linux stores not only the host name of the
           remote host, but its IP number as well. This option translates the
           IP number back into a hostname.

       --ff, ----ffiillee _f_i_l_e
           Tell llaasstt to use a specific _f_i_l_e instead of _/_v_a_r_/_l_o_g_/_w_t_m_p. The
           ----ffiillee option can be given multiple times, and all of the specified
           files will be processed.

       --FF, ----ffuullllttiimmeess
           Print full login and logout times and dates.

       --ii, ----iipp
           Like ----ddnnss ,, but displays the host’s IP number instead of the name.

       --_n_u_m_b_e_r; --nn, ----lliimmiitt _n_u_m_b_e_r
           Tell llaasstt how many lines to show.

       --pp, ----pprreesseenntt _t_i_m_e
           Display the users who were present at the specified time. This is
           like using the options ----ssiinnccee and ----uunnttiill together with the same
           _t_i_m_e.

       --RR, ----nnoohhoossttnnaammee
